    .{ .slug = "input-autocomplete-onoff", .title = "autocomplete attribute: on & off values", .search = "autocomplete attribute: on & off values input-autocomplete-onoff", .description = "The autocomplete attribute for input elements indicates to the browser whether a value should or should not be autofilled when appropriate.", .notes = "This support information does not include support for other autocomplete values. As described in detail below, many modern browsers ignore the off value on certain fields in certain cases intentionally in order to give the user more control over autofilling fields. One example is the use of password managers.", .summary = .{ "154 Partial (#2)", "156 Partial (#3)", "27 Partial (#5)", "152 Partial (#2)" }, .details = .{ "Version history: 4–16: No; 17–26: Full; 27–40: Partial (#1); 41–154: Partial (#2).\n#1: Partial support refers to ignoring the off value for password fields. see related blog post\n#2: Partial support in Chrome refers to the browser intentionally ignoring autocomplete=\"off\" when the user uses the browser's autofill functionality. see bug", "Version history: 2–29: Full; 30–156: Partial (#3).\n#3: Partial support in Firefox refers to ignoring autocomplete=\"off\" for login forms. see bug", "Version history: 3.1–5: No; 5.1–6.1: Full; 7–27: Partial (#5).\n#5: Safari ignores the off value for username, email and password fields", "Version history: 12–18: Partial (#1); 79–152: Partial (#2).\n#1: Partial support refers to ignoring the off value for password fields. see related blog post\n#2: Partial support in Chrome refers to the browser intentionally ignoring autocomplete=\"off\" when the user uses the browser's autofill functionality. see bug" } },
